WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.975
Chapter 08's architecture
is simple to draw.

2
00:00:02.975 --> 00:00:07.933
A branch query goes through the SD-WAN
edge to a network-addressable agency

3
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:12.251
resolver, into the policy plane
for policy, response policy zones,

4
00:00:12.251 --> 00:00:15.850
DNSSEC and IPAM, and on to
the per-cloud private zones.

5
00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:20.819
Internet-bound recursion leaves
through CISA's Protective DNS Resolver,

6
00:00:20.819 --> 00:00:25.788
which blocks, redirects or sinkholes
queries matching threat indicators.

7
00:00:25.788 --> 00:00:29.534
On-premises clients reach
the agency resolver directly;

8
00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:34.476
cloud workloads reach the plane through
Chapter 07's per-cloud forwarding.

9
00:00:34.476 --> 00:00:40.000
Five decisions carry almost all of the
operational risk, and each fails silently:

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.700
the query still resolves, just outside
policy or to the wrong address.

11
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.271
So each has a check to run
in the live configuration,

12
00:00:48.271 --> 00:00:52.437
not the design document, before
the next SD-WAN or DDI change.

13
00:00:52.437 --> 00:00:57.698
Decision 1, the redirect-dns destination:
a DDI resolver or forwarding

14
00:00:57.698 --> 00:01:02.051
proxy at the branch or a regional
hub, never a loopback stub.

15
00:01:02.051 --> 00:01:04.662
Check every branch's running data policy.

16
00:01:04.662 --> 00:01:08.425
Decision 2, forwarding chain
order: a SASE resolver is a

17
00:01:08.425 --> 00:01:13.087
forwarding target of the agency
resolver, never the redirect target.

18
00:01:13.087 --> 00:01:16.019
The common failure is
every component present

19
00:01:16.019 --> 00:01:18.950
with the SASE service
first, so trace the order.

20
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:23.526
Decision 3, per-cloud conditional
forwarding: the plane holds every

21
00:01:23.526 --> 00:01:28.675
private namespace's rule and pushes it
to every resolver, branches included.

22
00:01:28.675 --> 00:01:32.511
Check: resolve one name per
namespace from on-premises,

23
00:01:32.511 --> 00:01:37.625
each cloud and a branch; a public
address or NXDOMAIN means a missing rule.

24
00:01:37.625 --> 00:01:43.684
Decision 4, the AD DNS inventory, reprovisioned
before any domain controller goes,

25
00:01:43.684 --> 00:01:49.387
because once the last one holding a zone
is gone, its records can't be recovered.

26
00:01:49.387 --> 00:01:52.950
Check for a dated, reconciled
export before approval.

27
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:58.150
Decision 5, OCSP and CRL
reachability from every origin.

28
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:04.080
Check: resolve each endpoint in the issuing
CA certificates, and fetch a response.
